WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.072
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book two.

2
00:00:05.372 --> 00:00:09.095
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.395 --> 00:00:16.598
Unit five: Global Governance. Today you will discuss institutions using complex passive constructions.

4
00:00:16.898 --> 00:00:19.643
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:23.937
Part one: words. You will learn ten new words.

6
00:00:24.783 --> 00:00:25.947
Supranational.

7
00:00:26.950 --> 00:00:31.016
Supranational means having authority that goes beyond individual nations.

8
00:00:31.617 --> 00:00:34.882
The European Union is a supranational organisation.

9
00:00:35.483 --> 00:00:37.938
Your turn. Say: Supranational.

10
00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:41.998
Supranational.

11
00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:44.761
Multilateral institution.

12
00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:50.989
Multilateral institution means an organisation in which several states cooperate.

13
00:00:51.583 --> 00:00:55.150
The United Nations is the best-known multilateral institution.

14
00:00:55.750 --> 00:00:59.308
Your turn. Say: Multilateral institution.

15
00:01:02.167 --> 00:01:04.259
Multilateral institution.

16
00:01:05.017 --> 00:01:06.227
Global commons.

17
00:01:07.217 --> 00:01:11.759
Global commons means resources shared by all, such as the oceans and atmosphere.

18
00:01:12.350 --> 00:01:15.116
The global commons belong to no single nation.

19
00:01:15.717 --> 00:01:18.392
Your turn. Say: Global commons.

20
00:01:21.233 --> 00:01:22.710
Global commons.

21
00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:25.136
Transnational governance.

22
00:01:26.150 --> 00:01:31.284
Transnational governance means rule-making that crosses borders and involves non-state actors.

23
00:01:31.883 --> 00:01:35.253
Transnational governance now regulates shipping and the internet.

24
00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:38.978
Your turn. Say: Transnational governance.

25
00:01:41.833 --> 00:01:43.705
Transnational governance.

26
00:01:44.450 --> 00:01:45.857
Sovereignty erosion.

27
00:01:46.850 --> 00:01:51.264
Sovereignty erosion means the gradual loss of a state's independent authority.

28
00:01:51.850 --> 00:01:56.357
Critics fear sovereignty erosion when treaties limit national choices.

29
00:01:56.950 --> 00:01:59.916
Your turn. Say: Sovereignty erosion.

30
00:02:02.767 --> 00:02:04.232
Sovereignty erosion.

31
00:02:04.983 --> 00:02:06.310
Global regime.

32
00:02:07.317 --> 00:02:11.894
Global regime means a set of rules and norms that guides states in one area.

33
00:02:12.483 --> 00:02:15.946
The global regime on trade is overseen by the WTO.

34
00:02:16.550 --> 00:02:19.597
Your turn. Say: Global regime.

35
00:02:22.433 --> 00:02:23.771
Global regime.

36
00:02:24.517 --> 00:02:26.098
Collective action problem.

37
00:02:27.083 --> 00:02:31.974
Collective action problem means a situation where individual self-interest harms the group.

38
00:02:32.583 --> 00:02:35.512
Climate change is a classic collective action problem.

39
00:02:36.117 --> 00:02:39.675
Your turn. Say: Collective action problem.

40
00:02:42.533 --> 00:02:44.266
Collective action problem.

41
00:02:45.017 --> 00:02:46.981
Institutional legitimacy.

42
00:02:47.983 --> 00:02:53.187
Institutional legitimacy means the widely accepted right of an institution to make decisions.

43
00:02:53.783 --> 00:02:57.838
Institutional legitimacy depends on fairness and transparency.

44
00:02:58.450 --> 00:03:01.950
Your turn. Say: Institutional legitimacy.

45
00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:06.707
Institutional legitimacy.

46
00:03:07.450 --> 00:03:08.915
Accountability gap.

47
00:03:09.917 --> 00:03:14.993
Accountability gap means a lack of effective ways to hold decision-makers responsible.

48
00:03:15.583 --> 00:03:19.557
An accountability gap emerged when no body could sanction offenders.

49
00:03:20.150 --> 00:03:23.360
Your turn. Say: Accountability gap.

50
00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:27.619
Accountability gap.

51
00:03:28.383 --> 00:03:29.373
Treaty body.

52
00:03:30.383 --> 00:03:34.159
Treaty body means a committee that monitors how states follow a treaty.

53
00:03:34.750 --> 00:03:37.945
A treaty body reviews each country's human rights record.

54
00:03:38.550 --> 00:03:41.411
Your turn. Say: Treaty body.

55
00:03:44.267 --> 00:03:45.407
Treaty body.

56
00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:49.920
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

57
00:03:50.783 --> 00:03:54.664
Visal, why can't countries simply solve climate change on their own?

58
00:03:55.750 --> 00:04:05.275
Because it is a collective action problem. The global commons must be managed through shared institutions, or each state free-rides.

59
00:04:06.383 --> 00:04:09.114
Then why not give those institutions real power?

60
00:04:10.217 --> 00:04:18.848
Sovereignty is zealously guarded. It is feared that supranational authority would be imposed on nations that have never consented to it.

61
00:04:19.950 --> 00:04:21.183
So we are stuck?

62
00:04:22.283 --> 00:04:33.562
Not entirely. Legitimacy can be built if decisions are made transparently, and if accountability gaps are closed by treaty bodies that can actually be heard.

63
00:04:34.700 --> 00:04:36.491
Part three: grammar.

64
00:04:37.350 --> 00:04:40.789
Grammar focus: Complex passive constructions.

65
00:04:41.089 --> 00:04:42.392
Look at the pattern.

66
00:04:43.083 --> 00:04:46.523
The global commons must be managed through shared institutions.

67
00:04:47.417 --> 00:04:50.241
It has been argued that sovereignty is being eroded.

68
00:04:51.150 --> 00:04:53.939
Decisions ought to have been made more transparently.

69
00:04:54.900 --> 00:05:04.089
Combine modal + passive: must be managed, should have been resolved, is being eroded. The structure packs time, obligation and responsibility into one verb group.

70
00:05:04.850 --> 00:05:09.720
Be careful! Don't say: The commons must manage through institutions.

71
00:05:11.667 --> 00:05:15.501
Say: The commons must be managed through institutions.

72
00:05:15.801 --> 00:05:18.729
Modal passive: must be + p.p.

73
00:05:19.533 --> 00:05:22.279
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

74
00:05:23.067 --> 00:05:24.451
Who Governs the World?.

75
00:05:24.751 --> 00:05:32.927
No world government exists, yet cross-border problems such as pandemics, pollution and financial crises demand coordinated responses.

76
00:05:33.933 --> 00:05:41.250
Multilateral institutions have therefore been created, and global regimes have been negotiated to govern trade, health and the oceans.

77
00:05:42.267 --> 00:05:49.351
Because the global commons cannot be protected by any single state, collective action problems must be solved through binding agreements.

78
00:05:50.367 --> 00:05:56.987
However, it is often claimed that sovereignty is being eroded, and legitimacy is frequently questioned when accountability gaps appear.

79
00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:06.234
Whether global institutions can be reformed without being captured by powerful states remains one of the central questions of international politics.

80
00:06:07.333 --> 00:06:11.416
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

81
00:06:12.250 --> 00:06:15.085
The commons blank managed through shared institutions.

82
00:06:15.385 --> 00:06:19.585
A, must be. B, must. C, must been.

83
00:06:23.133 --> 00:06:25.144
The answer is A: must be.

84
00:06:26.283 --> 00:06:28.887
Resources shared by all nations are the blank.

85
00:06:29.187 --> 00:06:33.362
A, treaty body. B, global commons. C, regime.

86
00:06:36.900 --> 00:06:39.759
The answer is B: global commons.

87
00:06:40.917 --> 00:06:44.646
A situation where self-interest harms the group is a blank problem.

88
00:06:44.946 --> 00:06:49.424
A, supranational. B, collective action. C, legitimate.

89
00:06:52.967 --> 00:06:55.326
The answer is B: collective action.

90
00:06:56.483 --> 00:06:58.193
Choose the correct passive.

91
00:06:58.493 --> 00:07:06.918
A, It has been argued that rules should reform.. B, It has argued that rules should be reformed.. C, It has been argued that rules should be reformed..

92
00:07:10.467 --> 00:07:14.359
The answer is C: It has been argued that rules should be reformed..

93
00:07:15.517 --> 00:07:18.515
According to the reading, legitimacy is questioned when…

94
00:07:18.815 --> 00:07:24.895
A, institutions are transparent. B, accountability gaps appear. C, treaties are signed.

95
00:07:28.433 --> 00:07:31.605
The answer is B: accountability gaps appear.

96
00:07:32.800 --> 00:07:36.079
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

97
00:07:36.917 --> 00:07:40.693
Can global problems be solved without stronger global governance?

98
00:07:57.483 --> 00:07:58.914
Now listen to Visal.

99
00:07:59.214 --> 00:08:34.371
I doubt that serious global problems can be solved without stronger governance, although it must be built carefully. Climate change shows why: every country benefits if others cut emissions, so voluntary action stalls. Binding agreements and credible institutions are needed to overcome that collective action problem. However, such bodies must be accountable and transparent, or their legitimacy will erode. Stronger governance should therefore be combined with real democratic oversight.

100
00:08:34.671 --> 00:08:36.333
Excellent answer, Visal!

101
00:08:37.250 --> 00:08:41.853
Great work! Now you can use ten new words and complex passive constructions.

102
00:08:42.153 --> 00:08:47.243
Today's value is global cooperation. Shared problems need shared solutions.

103
00:08:47.543 --> 00:08:50.149
See you in Unit six. Goodbye!

104
00:08:50.449 --> 00:08:52.846
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

